Phase 1----------------------------------------------------------------------
-> Difference between MySQL and PostgresSQL?

My SQL- it is good for storing normal data types. it does not know about new datatype structures like JSON DB which prvoides a more powerful
way to work with JSON
-It does not Know anything about indexing and can lead to poor performance
- It is known for its performance with a good hardware and great configuration but can be affected by a poor database design

PostgredSQL- it supports new datatyes like JSON DB and enum
- It is useful in web application that handles large databases
- It is the best and efficient at indexing databases if use efficiently to create indexes


->What is a Database?

- A database is a collection of organized data that can be easily accessed, managaged and updated( it is like an organized notebook 
where every page has information written in a very structured way so that you can search, update and retrieve it easily)

- In more technical terms
. It stores data electronically
. The data is stored in terms of tables
. You can query the data using language to read, write, update and delete the data


-> What is RDBMS?

-RDBMS stands for Relational Database management system.
Relational- Databse is organized in tables
Database- collection of organized data
Management system- software that allows you to read, create, update and delete the data

So RDBMS is a software that allows you to store data in tables , relate them to each other and perform operation on them using SQL


-> so why PostgredSQL
. Open source
. Most feature rich and powerful
. Support complex queries like Join and window function
. you can make your own data types etc 
. support for NoSQl features


Schema- it is like a folder that helps organize you tables, views and functions etc , we can organize objects using schemas
Table- it is the main place where data is stored 
        . columns- defines the type of data(name, age, salary)
        . rows- each record


Phase 2------------------------------------------------------
Basic commanda-
CRUD operations-

> You can also create a new database using command line-
-> CREATE DATABASE new_db;

> To create the table you need to write a simple query like this.-
-> CREATE TABLE students(
    student_id INT,
    roll no. INT
    age INT,
    name VARCHAR(50)
);


> now we also have to fill the table cause there are only columns so for that a very simple insert query will do.
-> INSERT INTO students(roll no., age, name)
   VALUES(23,13,'Ankit'),
         (33,12,'Rahul');


> BASIC USE OF CRUD
-> SELECT * FROM students;  read the table.
-> SELECT name FROM students;  This will display all the columns of your data, and for specific column you can provide the name of that column.


> you can also do the conditional selection using the WHERE keyword in query.
->SELECT name from students WHERE grade='A';

> update the data in table
UPDATE students SET age=23 WHERE name='Akash';

> delete aswell using any column and the row will be removed.
-> DELETE FROM students WHERE name='Akash';


Phase 3-----------------------------------------------------------------------
Appropriate Data Type-
constraints-

If we talk about SQL we have multiple datatypes these
include.
1) Numeric Data Types
. INT
. NUMERIC(p,s)- exact precision
. REAL - 4 byte floating point
. SERIAL- auto increment

2) Character/String Data Types
. VARCHAR- variable length
. TEXT- unlimited length

3) Boolean Type
. boolean- TRUE< FALSE< NULL

4) Date and Time Types
. DATE- only Date
. TIME- only Time
. TIMESTAMP - date+Time
. TIMESTAMPTZ- date+time+zone

5) constraints
. PRIMARY KEY- uniquely identifies every row
. NOT NULL - column must have a VALUES
. UNIQUE- not duplicate VALUES
. DEFAULT - provide a default values if none
. CHECk- validates values
. FOREIGN KEY- links one table to another

practice--

CREATE DATABASE Flipkart_db;

CREATE TABLE products(
    Product_id SERIAL PRIMARY KEY,
    name VARCHAR(100) NOT NULL,
    sku_code char(8) UNIQUE NOT NULL,
    PRICE NUMERIC(10,2) NOT NULL,
    Stock_quantity INT CHECK(Stock_quantity>0),
    is_available Boolean DEFAULT TRUE,
    Category TEXT NOT NULL,
    Added_on DATETIME DEFAULT current_date,
    Last_updated TIMEZONE DEFAULT now()
);


Phase 4-------------------------------------------------------------------------
-> Clauses
-> Operators and Aggregation functions

clauses- clause are mainly used for querying the data and are find of building blocks

1. SELECT - choose columns to display
2. FROM- specify the table
3. WHERE - filter rows based on conditions
4. GROUP BY- groups rows for aggregation(Process of combining multiple rows in a single statistic)
5. Having- filter aggregated groups use after GROUP BY
6. LIMIT- limits the no. of rows returned
7. AS - rename column or tables tempararily
8. DISTINCT- return only unique distinct data

Q1. Show the name and price of all products.
-> SELECT name, price from products ;

Q2. Show all products where the category is 'Electronics'.
-> SELECT * from products WHERE category='Electronics';

Q3. Group products by category. Show each category once.
-> SELECT category from products GROUP BY category;

Q4. Show categories that have more than 1 product. (Use after GROUP BY)
-> SELECT category, count(*) from products GROUP BY category HAVING count(*)>1;

Q5. Show all products sorted by price in ascending order.
-> SELECT * from products ORDER BY price ASC;

Q6. Show only the fi rst 3 products from the table.
-> SELECT * from products LIMIT 3;

Q7. Show product name as "Item_Name" and price as "Item_Price".
-> SELECT name AS "Item_Name", price AS "Item_Price" from products;

Q8. Show all the unique categories from the products table.
-> SELECT DISTINCT category from products;


-> Clauses with Operrators ------------

select * from products;

Select * from products where category != 'Electronics';

Select * from products where price < 1000 and category='Electronics';

select * from products where price BETWEEN 400 and 1000;

select * from products where category IN ('Electronics', 'Home & Kitchen', 'Fitness');  -- IN for multiple conditions

select * from products where sku_code LIKE 'W%'; -- % for multiple words

select * from products where sku_code LIKE '%123%';

select * from products where sku_code LIKE '_B%';  -- _ means one space of words


-> Aggregation Functions-------------

1. count() - count no. of rows
2. sum() - add numeric values
3. avg() - calculate the average
4. min() - find smallest value
5. max() - find highest value

select count(product_id) from products;

select sum(price) from products where category = 'Electronics';

select avg(price) from products;

select round(avg(price),2) from products;  -- round can handle the number of values after the decimal 


-- Exercise of clauses with operators & aggregation functions

select name, price from products where price=(select min(price) from products);

select avg(price) as avg_price from products where category IN ('Home & Kitchen', 'Fitness') Group BY category; 

select name , stock_quantity from products where is_available=True AND stock_quantity>50 AND price!=299;

select max(price), category from products GROUP BY category;

select distinct upper(category) as category_upper from products ORDER BY category_upper DESC;


-> String Functions : It is used to manipulate text data - like names, categories etc;
-They help you
. Clean Text
. Extract parts of a String
. Convert clauses
. Replace or remove characters

i. First set of functions are Upper Lower and length function.

-> SELECT UPPER(name) FROM products;
-> SELECT LOWER(sku_code) FROM products;
-> SELECT LENGTH(name) FROM products;


ii. lets see substring - it is used to extract some portion from your string .
substring( text , location , length)

-> SELECT name, SUBSTRING(sku_code,1,2) FROM products;


iii. LEFT() and RIGHT() both have the use case similar to substring Get n leftmost and rightmost elements

-> SELECT LEFT(name,2) FROM products;

-> SELECT RIGHT(name,3) FROM products;


iv. Some other functions are Concat() - Concat joins 2 strings together.

-> SELECT CONCAT(name,' ',category) FROM products; --1st method

-> SELECT CONCAT_WS(' : ',name, category, price, is_available) FROM products; -- 2nd method for ease


v. now lets see trim() and replace()
trim() - This function will remove all the spaces from the string.
replace() - This function will replace any thing you want

-> SELECT TRIM('   Brothe   ') ;

-> SELECT name, replace(sku_code, left(sku_code,2),'GG') FROM products;

-> SELECT name ,replace(category, left(category,4),'LIT') FROM products;



Phase 5-----------------------------------------------------------

1. ALTER -----------------

CREATE TABLE student(
student_id SERIAL PRIMARY KEY,
name VARCHAR(100),
age INT
);

INSERT INTO student(name, age)
VALUES('Akarsh',24),
	  ('Anjali',23);

SELECT * FROM student;

i. Add a New Column
-> ALTER TABLE student ADD column email VARCHAR(100);

ii. Remove a Column
-> ALTER TABLE student DROP COLUMN email;

iii. Rename a Column.
-> ALTER TABLE student RENAME COLUMN name TO full_name;

iv. Change Data Type of a Column
-> ALTER TABLE student ALTER COLUMN age TYPE INT;

v. Set a Default Value.
-> ALTER TABLE student ALTER COLUMN age SET DEFAULT 19;

vi. Remove a Default Value
-> ALTER TABLE student ALTER COLUMN age DROP DEFAULT;

vii. ADD A CONSTRAINT
-> ALTER TABLE student ADD CONSTRAINT age_check CHECK(age>=19);

viii. DROP A CONSTRAINT
-> ALTER TABLE student DROP CONSTRAINT age_check;

ix. RENAME THE TABLE
ALTER TABLE student RENAME TO school_student;




